INT. CORNER LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

A long fluorescent room that hums on a note slightly flat of pleasant. Forty-year-old dryers turn their round windows to the room like patient, soapy eyes. Linoleum the color of weak tea. A vending machine sells one kind of detergent and one kind of regret.

At the far end, a wall intercom, brown with age, crackles every so often like a throat clearing itself.

THEODORA "TEDDY" MAKINDE-LOWE (60s, silver hair pinned under a scarf, the posture of a retired headmistress) folds towels with military corners. She does it slowly. She does it as an argument.

At the next folding table, GUILLERMINA "MINA" ASHWORTH-REYES (60s, jewelry that clinks, a laugh kept in reserve) is unloading a basket of what appear to be fifty identical white shirts.

Neither woman looks at the other. The dryers turn.

MINA
They've changed the soap.

TEDDY
They have not.

MINA
It smells different.

TEDDY
You smell different. You've changed your perfume.

MINA
I have not.

TEDDY
It has a note of disappointment in it.

Mina sets down a shirt, very gently, as one sets down a grenade. She smooths it. She picks up another.

MINA
Is that a towel or a tribute?

TEDDY
It is a towel folded the way a towel is meant to be folded.

MINA
It's a bit sharp for a towel. She'd never fold them like that, you know.

TEDDY
She would not. She didn't have the discipline. She had charm, which is the next best thing to a fully ironed shirt.

Mina's hands go still on the shirt in front of her.

MINA
Don't.

TEDDY
I merely said...

MINA
Don't do the voice. Don't do the voice you do.

TEDDY
What voice? This is my voice. It has been my voice since nineteen-sixty-one.

The wall intercom CRACKLES. Both women look up.

ATTENDANT (V.O.)
(over intercom, bored)
Attention customers. Dryer six is finished. Dryer six is finished. Please remove your items from dryer six.

Neither moves. Dryer six, at the end of the row, gives a long, low buzz.

TEDDY
That's yours.

MINA
Yours.

TEDDY
You were by it.

MINA
You were nearer.

ATTENDANT (V.O.)
(over intercom)
Dryer six. Also, someone has left a casserole dish in dryer three.

Teddy goes pink at the ears. Mina notices. She smiles, slowly, like a woman who has just found the pearl in the oyster.

MINA
A casserole dish.

TEDDY
It's nothing.

MINA
In a dryer.

TEDDY
It had gone cold, and I thought...

MINA
You thought you'd warm it up in a dryer. At a laundromat. At midnight.

TEDDY
(with dignity)
I was having an evening.

Mina starts to laugh. It's a small laugh at first, a hiccup behind her teeth. She presses a hand over her mouth. The bracelets clink.

TEDDY (CONT'D)
Stop that.

MINA
I'm not doing anything.

TEDDY
You're laughing at me.

MINA
I'm laughing at a casserole.

TEDDY
It was her recipe.

The laughter stops as if a switch had been thrown.

The dryers keep turning. The room hums its flat note. Mina picks a shirt up, puts it down. Teddy folds a towel, unfolds it, folds it again.

MINA
(quietly)
It was her recipe.

TEDDY
It's the only one I have.

MINA
I have the chicken.

TEDDY
You have the chicken.

MINA
I've had it for six years and I've never once made it.

TEDDY
Nor I the casserole. Until tonight. I put it in the dryer and I stood there and I watched it go round and I thought, this is the stupidest thing I have ever done.

MINA
It is the stupidest thing.

TEDDY
It is.

MINA
It's wonderful.

TEDDY
(cracking)
Mina, I cannot do this to a perfectly good evening.

She bangs a folded towel down on the table. A second. A third. She grabs the stack and hurls the whole thing across the room. It hits the vending machine with a soft, ridiculous FLUMP.

Mina stares. Then she picks up a shirt and throws it, too. It lands on Teddy's head. For a moment Teddy sits there under it, motionless, like a ghost in a bad school play.

TEDDY (CONT'D)
(from under the shirt)
That was hers.

MINA
Was it?

TEDDY
It was a very nice shirt.

She pulls it off. Both women are laughing now, helplessly, hanging onto the folding table.

ATTENDANT (V.O.)
(over intercom, deadpan)
Customers. Please keep your grief to the back of the store.

That makes it worse. They have to sit down on the floor.
